#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5256297-c928-54e3-bf4d-35f5734f5632" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfd612a0-8d9c-551c-a83d-74c0e3cc77e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{3}{v}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351031} {:answer/id #uuid "d28bdea0-7ab5-5903-8698-20f181cb2809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{3}{v}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351032} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9f6a1b-525a-5554-a40b-8b6a28f43944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{4}{v}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351033} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5a051a-6f4f-589f-b410-0ec4c3589f0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{5}{v}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351034} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4154ce-01ce-5b7b-87c6-a5a74439cad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{2}{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351035} {:answer/id #uuid "6791b055-8e8e-5390-bc51-c28f49dd0783" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{4}{v}^{4}|u|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6791b055-8e8e-5390-bc51-c28f49dd0783" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{4}{v}^{4}|u|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351171} :answer-field/id #uuid "57e0241f-39f2-5746-aa50-6d39b2135aef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c502543-bf1e-5bea-aad2-6cc3e7e40f67" :question/math-academy-id "q-161134" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,v,\\,v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ Use the scalar surface-area factor $\\|r_u\\times r_v\\|$ with its absolute values for the stated parameter domain. What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8}|u|.$*" :question/worked-solution "The scalar surface-area factor is nonnegative. For $r(u,v)=(u^2,v,v)$,\n\n$$r_u=(2u,0,0),\\qquad r_v=(0,1,1),\\qquad r_u\\times r_v=(0,-2u,2u).$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\\|r_u\\times r_v\\|=\\sqrt{8u^2}=\\sqrt8\\,|u|.$$\n\nSubstitution into $f(x,y,z)=x^2y^2z^2$ gives\n\n$$f(r(u,v))=(u^2)^2v^2v^2=u^4v^4.$$\n\nThe missing integrand for the stated general parameter domain is therefore\n\n$$\\sqrt8\\,u^4v^4|u|.$$\n\nThe original hint and source key used $\\sqrt8\\,u$ and $\\sqrt8\\,u^5v^4$, respectively, without restricting $u$. They are valid for $u\\ge0$ but fail for negative $u$. At $(u,v)=(-1,1)$ the correct integrand is $\\sqrt8$; the signed source expression gives $-\\sqrt8$, although the scalar function is nonnegative. This correction retains the original general domain, fixes the hint, and adds the absolute-value choice.\n\nThe parametrization loses rank at $u=0$. The displayed surface-integral equality has the usual requirement of regular patches and no multiple coverage except on negligible boundaries; the local area factor remains $\\sqrt8|u|$."}]}
